#. Tag: title
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Working with tablets"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This tutorial is intended to describe you the first steps with working with "
"a tablet with &krita;. The tutorial assumes you are using &Linux;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:8
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuring it"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:10
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As any hardware it nearly works out of the box. &Linux; should recognize it "
"fine, but you might have to configure the X11 server by hand. The best way "
"to do this is to follow the instruction on the Wacom &Linux; howto: <ulink "
"url=\"http://linuxwacom.sourceforge.net/index.php/howto/main\"></ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:14
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Then, in &krita;, you need to enable the various tools (in the "
"<guilabel>Tablet</guilabel> section of the <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure &krita;...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> dialog) "
"&mdash; you can find more information in the <link linkend=\"settings-"
"preferences-tablet\">tablet settings section</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:26
#, no-c-format
msgid "First contact with the tablet"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:28
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are three devices of your tablet that you can use with &krita;:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:33
#, no-c-format
msgid "the cursor, the mouse that was shipped with the Wacom tablet"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:35
#, no-c-format
msgid "the eraser, the round part on the top of the pen"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid "the stylus, the thin point on the bottom of the pen"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:39
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"By default, when you use the stylus or the cursor on the tablet, the Brush "
"tool and the pixel brush painting operation will get selected. The eraser "
"device is associated to the <quote>pixel eraser</quote> painting operation. "
"But if you select a different tool or a different painting operation with "
"one device, &krita; will remember the association when you switch between "
"devices."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:50
#, no-c-format
msgid "Outlines of a flower"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Even if you knew how to draw before you started with a tablet, you will need "
"to adapt to the tablet. It doesn't feel the same. So I suggest to start with "
"something simple, like a flower, and to use a picture as a model:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>A flower</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:66
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>A flower</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>A flower</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"First, you will need to create a new layer for the outline. I advise you to "
"lock the layer with the picture, it will prevent you from making mistakes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Drawing the outline of the flower seems pretty easy, but for your first "
"experience you will have a great difficulty to precisely follow the line on "
"the screen while your hand has to move on the tablet. Eventually you will "
"get something like this:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:85
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The outline of the flower</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:91
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The outline of the flower</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The outline of the flower</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid "Colorization"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For the colorization, you will need to create a third layer. You will have "
"to move it below the layer with the outlines, and do not forget to lock the "
"outline layer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It's mostly easier than the outline part, just select the color you want to "
"use (either with the color selector or with the color picker), then for most "
"of the work you can use the fill tool: with the mouse, click on the part you "
"want to fill, as by default the fill tool will take the outline into "
"consideration. On the following image, the different colors of the heart of "
"the flower are not separated by outlines, to do them I just completed the "
"missing outline with a yellow or brown line to create the separation between "
"the different colors."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:120
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The colored flower</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The colored flower</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:128
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The colored flower</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tutorial-tablet.docbook:133
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The resulting image looks and feels like old fashion clipart, mostly because "
"it lacks shadows and illumination, which are not covered by this tutorial."
msgstr ""
